In the staging of oral cavity cancer, are radiographic findings alone sufficient to diagnose extranodal extension (ENE)?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","No, radiographic findings alone are insufficient to diagnose extranodal extension (ENE) in oral cavity cancer. While imaging (e.g., CT, MRI) can provide suggestive features, ENE is a clinical diagnosis that requires surgical or microscopic confirmation.  

- **Imaging findings (CT/MRI) can suggest ENE but are nonspecific:**  
  - **CT:** May show lymph node enlargement, asymmetry, or abnormal tissue extending beyond the lymph node hilum.  
  - **MRI:** May show a more detailed view of the lymph node and surrounding structures, potentially showing soft tissue involvement.  

- **Limitations of imaging:**  
  - **False positives:**  
  - **Lymph node enlargement** can be due to infections or other conditions.  
  - **Artifacts** or imaging artifacts may mimic ENE.  
  - **False negatives:**  
  - ENE may be subtle or involve structures not well visualized on imaging.  
  - Some ENE may be confined to the lymph node capsule, which may not be visible.  

- **Clinical diagnosis:**  
  - ENE is confirmed by **histopathology** (microscopic examination) of the lymph node or surrounding structures.  
  - **Surgical or pathologic examination** confirms the presence of tumor cells beyond the lymph node capsule.  

Thus, imaging helps guide surgical planning and assess the extent of disease, but it cannot definitively diagnose ENE.  

$\boxed{\text{Imaging (CT/MRI) is suggestive but not diagnostic; ENE requires surgical/pathologic confirmation.}}$",relevance,"# Task Description
