To calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction, we use the equation:

ΔH = ΔH₀(products) - ΔH₀(reactants)

where ΔH₀ is the standard enthalpy of formation.

For the given reaction:

\[
\text{CaO(s) + H}_2\text{O(l)} \rightarrow \text{Ca(OH)}_2\text{(s)}
\]

The standard enthalpy of formation of:
- \( \text{Ca(OH)}_2(s] = -986.2 \, \text{kJ/mol}
- \( \text{H}_2\text{O}(l) = -285.8 \, \text{kJ/mol}
- \( \text{CaO}(s] = -635.1 \, \text{kJ/mol}

Substituting these values into the equation:

\[
\Delta H = \DeltaH₀(\text{Ca(OH)}_2) - \DeltaH₀(\text{CaO}) - \DeltaH₀(\text{H}_2\text{O}]
\]
\[
\Delta H = (-986.2 \, \text{kJ/mol}) - (-635.1 \, \text{kJ/mol}) - (-285.8 \, \text{kg/mol})
\]
\[
\Delta H = -986.2 + 635.1 + 281.1 = -1000.2 \, \text{kJ/mol}
\]

Thus, the standard enthalpy change for the reaction is \(\boxed{-1000.2 \, \text{kJ/mol}}\).
