","To solve this problem, we can use the concept of the normal normal distribution. The total number of houses is 90.
Number of houses with a two-car garage = 50.
Number of houses with an in-the-ground swimming pool = 40.
Number of houses with neither a two-car garage nor an in-the-ground swimming pool = 35.

The number of houses with either a two-car garage or an in-the-ground swimming pool is the total number of houses minus the number of houses with neither.
Number of houses with either a two-car garage or an in-the-ground swimming pool = 90 - 35 = 55.

Using the formula for the sum of two variables:
Number of houses with a two-car garage or an in-the-ground swimming pool = Number of houses with a two-car garage + Number of houses with an in-the-ground swimming pool - Number of houses with both a two-car garage and an in-the-ground swimming pool.
\[
\text{Number of houses with a two-car garage or an in-the-ground swimming pool} = \text{Number of houses with a two-car garage} + \text{Number of houses with an in-the-ground swimming pool} - \text{Number of houses with both}
\]
\[
55 = 50 + 40 - \text{Number of houses with both}
\]
\[
55 = 90 - \text{Number of houses with both}
\]
\[
\text{Number of houses with both} = 90 - 55 = 35
\]

Thus, there are 35 houses with both a two-car garage and an in-the-ground swimming pool.

