1. **Distance a cycles before b starts**:  
   a cycles for 3 hours at 10 kmph, so he travels \(3 \times 10 = 30\) km.

2. **Time for b to catch up**:  
   b cycles faster (20 kmph) than a (10 kmph), so he gains 10 km per hour on a.  
   To catch up by 30 km, it will take \(30 \times 1 = 3\) hours.

3. **Distance b cycles to catch up**:  
   During these 3 hours, b cycles \(20 \times 3 = 60\) km.

4. **Distance from the start**:  
