"Calculate the change in Gibbs free energy when 2 moles of hydrogen gas react with 1 mole of oxygen gas at a constant temperature of 298 K and a pressure of 1 bar, given the standard Gibbs free energy of formation of water to be -237.1 kJ/mol.","<bos><start_of_turn>user
Calculate the change in Gibbs free energy when 2 moles of hydrogen gas react with 1 mole of oxygen gas at a constant temperature of 298 K and a pressure of 1 bar, given the standard Gibbs free energy of formation of water to be -237.1 kJ/mol.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To calculate the change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction, we use the Gibbs free energy change equation:  
\[
\DeltaG = n \DeltaG_{app,0}
\]  
where:  
- \(\DeltaG\) is the Gibbs free energy change for the given conditions.  
- \(n\) is the number of moles of the product (water in this case).  
- \(\DeltaG_{ap.0}\) is the standard Gibbs free energy change per mole of the product.  

The reaction is:  
\[
2 \text{H}_2 + \text{O}_2 \rightarrow 2 \text{H}_2\text{O}
\]  

Given:  
- Standard Gibbs free energy of formation of water (\(\Delta G_{app.0}\)) = -237.1 kJ/mol  
- Number of moles of water formed (\(n\)) = 2 moles  

Substituting the values:  
\[
\DeltaG = 2 \times (-237.1 \, \text{kJ/mol}) = -464.2 \, \text{kJ}
\]  

Thus, the change in Gibbs free energy is \(\boxed{-464.2 \, \text{kJ}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
