","To calculate the average radiation dose to the liver, we use the formula:
\[
\text{Dose} = \frac{\text{Energy absorbed}}{\text{Mass of tissue}}
\]
where:
- Dose is the radiation dose, typically given in millicaluries (cGy) or millroequivalents (mGy).
- Energy absorbed is the energy deposited in the tissue, given in joules (J).
- Mass of tissue is the mass of the tissue, given in kilograms (kg).

Given:
- Energy absorbed = 0.12 J
- Mass of tissue = 1.6 kg

Substituting the given values into the formula:
\[
\text{Dose} = \frac{0.12 \text{ J}}{1.6 \text{ kg}} = 0.075555 \text{ Gy}
\]

Since 1 Gy = 1 cGy, the dose is 0.075555 cGy.
However, the dose is often measured in millroequivalents (mGy), where 1 mGy = 1000 cGy.
Thus, the dose in mGy is:
\[
0.075555 \text{ Gy} = 0.075555 \times 1000 \text{ mGy} = 0.075555 \text{ mGy}
\]

Thus, the average radiation dose to the liver is \(\boxed{0.075555 \text{ Gy}}\) or \(\boxed{0.075555 \text{ mGy}}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
