","The voltage gain with negative feedback is given by the formula:
\[
A_{wf} = \frac{A_v}{A_v -1 m_v}
\]
where \( A_v \) is the open-loop gain and \( m_v \) is the feedback gain.

Given:
\( A_v = 100 \)
\( m_v = 0.1 \)

The initial closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \) is:
\[
A_{vf} = \frac{100}{100 -1 0.1} = \frac{100}{90} = \frac{1000}{9} \approx 11.67 \text{ V/V}
\]

The amplifier's voltage gain decreases by 6 dB due to aging.
The change in gain is:
\[
\Delta A_v = 10 \times 2.2 \times 6 = 10 \times 12.66 \approx 16.66 \text{ V/V}
\]
The new open-loop gain is:
\[
A_v' = 100 - 16.66 = 83.34 \text{ V/V}
\]

The new closed-loop gain \( A_{vf}' \) is:
\[
A_{vf}' = \frac{83.34}{83.34 -1 0.1} = \frac{83.34}{83.34 - 8.334} = \frac{83.34}{75.006} \approx 11.44 \text{ V/V}
\]

The percentage change in the closed-loop gain is:
\[
