Along the length of a constant area fin, the temperature gradient ____________.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","Along the length of a constant area fin, the temperature gradient **decreases** as the fin length increases. This is because the heat is transferred away from the heat source, causing the temperature to drop as the fin is longer.  

The temperature gradient is the change in temperature per unit length, and it is proportional to the heat flux (heat flow per unit area). As the fin is longer, the heat flux decreases as some heat has already been dissipated.  

Thus, the temperature gradient decreases with fin length.  

\[ \frac{dQ}{dQ_x} = \frac{dQ}{dQ_x} = \frac{Q}{L} = \frac{k \cdot A \cdot \Delta T}{ \sqrt{k \cdot A \cdot \pi \cdot \lambda}} = \frac{\Delta T}{\sqrt{\pi \lambda}} \]

