","The coefficient of restitution \( e \) is defined as the ratio of the relative velocities of the two bodies after the collision to the relative velocities before the collision. In this case, the ball and the wall are the two bodies. The velocity of the wall is constant and zero, so the relative velocities are the velocities of the ball before and after the collision. Thus,
\[ e = \frac{v_2}{v_1} = \frac{v_2}{v_1} = \frac{\Delta v}{\Delta v} = \frac{\Delta v}{\Delta v} \]
where \( v_1 \) is the velocity of the ball before the collision and \( v_2 \) is the velocity of the ball after the collision.

The coefficient of restitution is given as \( e = \frac{1}{3} \). Thus, \( v_2 = \frac{1}{3} v_1 \).
The kinetic energy of the ball before the collision is \( KE_1 = \frac{1}{2} m v_1 \), and the kinetic energy after the collision is \( KE_2 = \frac{1}{2} m v_2 \).
The change in kinetic energy is \( \Delta KE = KE_1 - KE_2 = \frac{1}{2} m v_1 - \frac{1}{2} m v_2 = \frac{1}{2} m v_1 - \frac{1}{2} m \left( \frac{1}{3} v_1 \right) = \frac{1}{2} m v_1 - \frac{1}{6} m v_1 = \frac{3}{6} m v_1 - \frac{1}{6} m v_1 = \frac{2}{6} m v_1 = \frac{1}{3} m v_1 \).
The fraction of kinetic energy lost is the change in kinetic energy divided by the initial kinetic energy:
\[ \frac{\Delta KE}{KE_1} = \frac{\frac{1}{3} m v_1}{\frac{1}{2} m v_1} = \frac{1/3}{1/2} = \frac{1}{3} \leftleftfrac{2}{1}\rightright = \frac{2}{3}. \]

Thus, the fraction of kinetic energy lost is \(\frac{2}{3}\).

The kinetic energy lost is:
\[ KE_1 - KE_2 = \frac{1}{2} m v_1 - \frac{1}{2} m \left( \frac{1}{3} v_1 \right) = \frac{1}{2} m v_1 - \frac{1}{6} m v_1 = \frac{3}{6} m v_1 - \frac{1}{6} m v_1 = \frac{2}{6} m v_1 = \frac{1}{3} m v_1. \]
The fraction of kinetic energy lost is:
\[ \frac{\text{Kinetic energy lost}}{\text{Initial kinetic energy}} = \frac{\frac{1}{3} m v_1}{\frac{1}{2} m v_1} = \frac{1/3}{1/2} = \frac{1}{3} \leftleftfrac{2}{1}\rightright = \frac{2}{3}. \]

